“One interesting tidbit is Alphabet had only $7.1 Billion quarterly depreciation expense with $321 Billion PP&E in 2Q’26. Meta, on the other hand, reported $6.4 Billion quarterly depreciation with just $226 Billion PP&E. So, Alphabet reported just ~$700 million incremental depreciation for almost $100 Billion incremental PP&E. I don’t know whether that speaks to Alphabet’s better efficiency of managing their PP&E or that Meta is too conservative in their estimates of useful life for its PP&E. Given how capital intensive these businesses are becoming, these arcane details can be very important over time which unfortunately would be hard to evaluate/judge from the outside.”

Well, the accounting argument might be intertwined with the efficiency argument. The reason their useful life estimates might be higher for certain PP&E assets is because they might manage the assets better. Like I said, I don’t know. But just $2.8 Bn (annualized) higher depreciation expense for 100 Bn higher PP&E balance (CIP is only $40 Bn higher for GOOGL) seems more than just timing.

